---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="center">
  <h2 p-effect="from above">
    🌐 <span class="main-gradient">WebMCP</span>
  </h2>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <ul class="more-margin">
    <li>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">W3C Community Group draft</span> edited by{" "}
      <span p-fragment p-effect="highlight">
        Google & Microsoft{" "}
      </span>
      to expose web app features to AI agents
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      Adds <code>document.modelContext</code> to the page — a
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> tool surface for in-browser agents</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      Agents stop <span class="main-sub-gradient">guessing from the UI</span> —
      the app exposes exactly what's possible
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      WebMCP brings MCP into the browser. Instead of screen scraping, a site
      registers its tools via document.modelContext and the agent invokes them
      directly — like turning a webpage into an MCP server, entirely
      client-side.
    </p>
    <p>
      Early builds exposed it as navigator.modelContext: Chrome 150 deprecated
      that alias in favour of document.modelContext. If someone saw older demos,
      that's why the name differs.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h5 class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">Registering a tool - <span class="accent">imperative</span> mode</span>
  </h5>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="app.ts">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-typescript">  await document.modelContext.registerTool({
    name: "search-products",
    description: "Search the product catalog by keyword",
    inputSchema: {
      type: "object",
      properties: {
        query: { type: "string", description: "Search term" }
      },
      required: ["query"]
    },
    annotations: { readOnlyHint: true },
</code><p-fragment><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-typescript">   execute: async ({ query }) => {
     const results = await api.search(query);
     return results;
   }
  });</code></p-fragment>
  </pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Same shape as a regular MCP tool — name, description, inputSchema.</p>
    <p>
      <code>annotations.readOnlyHint</code> tells the agent this tool doesn't
      change state, so it's safe to call without asking.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key difference is <code>execute</code>: it runs in the browser, with
      direct access to app state, the DOM, cookies — everything.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h5 class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">Registering a tool - <span class="accent">declarative</span> mode</span>
  </h5>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="search-form.html">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-html">  &lt;form
      toolname="search-products"
      tooldescription="Search the product catalog by keyword"
      action="/search"&gt;
        &lt;input
          name="query"
          type="text"
          toolparamdescription="Search term to filter products"
        /&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;</code>
  </pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      In declarative mode, we don't register the tool from JavaScript. We
      describe it directly in HTML.
    </p>
    <p>
      The form becomes the tool: <code>toolname</code> is the tool name,
      <code>tooldescription</code> explains what it does, and form fields become
      the input parameters.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is powerful because existing HTML semantics are reused instead of
      creating a completely separate integration layer.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h2 class="main-gradient" p-effect="from above">
    Where are we <span class="accent">now</span>?
  </h2>
  <ul class="more-margin">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      Origin trials started in
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> Chrome 149 & Edge 150</span> — still
      experimental
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      Tools are tied to the
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> live page/session</span> — built for
      browser workflows with a human in the loop
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> W3C Community Group draft</span> — no
      cross-browser consensus yet
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Still experimental — treat it as a preview, not a production standard.
      WebMCP complements backend MCP, it doesn't replace it: it shines when the
      user is on the page and the agent assists them in context.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chrome origin trial runs 149 → 156 (ends mid-November 2026). For local
      testing: chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing, no token needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Versions and dates shift — always re-check the status before quoting
      numbers on stage.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<style lang="scss">
  [p-effect~="highlight"] {
    background: linear-gradient(
        color-mix(
          in srgb,
          var(--ending-point-gradient, oklch(0.6 0.1 80deg)),
          transparent var(--highlight-transparency, 85%)
        )
      )
      0 / calc(100% * var(--fragment-progress)) auto no-repeat;
    filter: none;
    &[aria-hidden="false"] {
      border: 1px solid var(--ending-point-gradient);
    }
  }
</style>
